<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  sumo-gui view settings for the MIXED-TRAFFIC DEMO WATCH (CLAUDE.md §10/§11).

  Purpose: make the human sign-off watch reproducible, so the reviewer is not
  hand-setting the view every time and is not left squinting at vehicles too
  small to judge.

  REVISED 2026-09-02 after the first watch. Three things were wrong and all
  three are fixed here or in sim/run_demo_gui.py:

    1. BIKES WERE INVISIBLE. Two causes. (a) The view auto-fitted the whole
       900x300m corridor, at which scale a 1.8m x 0.65m bike is a few pixels.
       Fixed by defaulting the viewport to ONE junction — run_demo_gui.py
       writes the viewport, see 'focus'. (b) Colour was "by type", which hashes
       the type id to an arbitrary colour and gave bikes no visual priority.
       Fixed by explicit per-vType colours (see below) plus the matching
       colorScheme here.

    2. THE ROADS LOOKED CONGESTED. Measured, not guessed: that was the WATCH
       HARNESS, not the driving model. The old watch ran netconvert's static
       fixed-timer TLS, the dumbest controller in the project. Same corridor,
       same demand, same seed, only the controller swapped:
           fixed timer : 29.1% of vehicles halted, peak 60.4%, 21.1 km/h
           Tier 0      :  8.0% halted,             peak 15.5%, 29.2 km/h
       run_demo_gui.py now drives with Tier 0 by default. Demand was tested
       first and REFUTED as the cause: halted% sat at 34-35% at every density
       from full training load down to 35% of it, so cutting traffic does not
       decongest — it only removes bikes from the screen (19.6 -> 6.0 on road).

    3. Vehicles were hard to pick out at any zoom. Addressed by the viewport
       change in (1) rather than by drawing them bigger - see the exaggeration
       block below, and note watch 2 showed that oversizing them caused a worse
       problem than it solved.

  ===========================================================================
  COLOUR KEY - explicit per-vType colours, set in vehicle_types_demo.add.xml
  ===========================================================================
  The colorScheme below is "given vehicle/type/route color", NOT "by type", so
  the vType colours are what you see. Palette is by CLASS with intensity by
  TIER, which is exactly the two things this watch has to separate:

      TWO-WHEELERS   green    cautious (dark) -> normal (bright) -> aggressive (lime)
      AUTOS          amber    cautious (pale) -> normal          -> aggressive (orange)
      CARS           blue     normal          -> aggressive (deep blue)
      TRUCK          grey
      AMBULANCE      red

  So: anything GREEN is a two-wheeler. If the model is working you should see
  green filtering forward through stopped ambers and blues at a queue front,
  and the LIME ones doing it hardest.

  vType `color` is a RENDERING attribute only — it enters no car-following,
  lane-changing or junction model. Proven rather than asserted: check_fix.py
  reports the identical arrived count (1737) and collision count (0) before and
  after the colours were added, and a semantic XML diff shows every vType
  gained exactly one key (`color`) with nothing removed and nothing changed.

  ===========================================================================
  vehicle_exaggeration DEFAULTS TO 1.0 (TRUE SCALE) - revised after watch 2
  ===========================================================================
  Watch 2 reported "the wheels of the vehicles move ahead and the vehicle
  remains behind for almost all the vehicles". That is what exaggeration 4.0
  looks like at density: a 4.5m car is DRAWN 18m long and a 7.5m truck 30m,
  so a vehicle's body is drawn straight through the two or three vehicles
  genuinely in front of it. The small shape apparently "ahead" of a body is a
  DIFFERENT, SMALLER VEHICLE (bike or auto) that the oversized body is drawn
  over the top of. Nothing is detached and no vehicle is lagging its own
  wheels - the geometry was a rendering artifact of the exaggeration.

  So exaggeration now defaults to 1.0: every vehicle is drawn at its true
  length and width, and what you see is the real geometry. At junction zoom
  that is perfectly legible - at 'focus' J2 a bike is roughly 16 x 6 screen
  pixels and a car 40 x 16 - so the exaggeration was never needed there. It
  is only needed for the whole-corridor view, and run_demo_gui.py raises it
  to 2.5 automatically for 'focus' all.

  showBlinker is also now 0. Blinkers are drawn as small marks at vehicle
  corners, which adds a second set of "extra bits around each vehicle" at
  exactly the densities where the picture is already hard to read.

  Exaggeration scales drawn SIZE only; positions, lateral offsets and dynamics
  are never touched, so the lane-straddling and queue-front filtering being
  judged are real at any setting. But ANY value above 1.0 makes vehicles look
  overlapped when they are not, so at 1.0 what you see is what the simulation
  actually has - including real physical clearance.

  This file is DEMO-ONLY scaffolding, like vehicle_types_demo.add.xml itself.
  Nothing headless reads a GUI settings file at all.

  NOTE: run_demo_gui.py writes a COPY of this file with a <viewport> element
  appended for the requested 'focus'. Editing this file changes the defaults
  for every future watch; editing the generated copy changes nothing.
-->
<viewsettings>

    <!-- Playback speed. A sumo-gui WALL-CLOCK sleep between rendered steps,
         with ZERO effect on the simulation (CLAUDE.md §8, ITEM 3a). -->
    <delay value="250"/>

    <scheme name="psychoflow demo (mixed traffic)">

        <opengl antialiase="1" dither="0"/>
        <background backgroundColor="255,255,255"
                    showGrid="0" gridXSize="100.00" gridYSize="100.00"/>

        <edges laneEdgeMode="0" scaleMode="0" laneShowBorders="1"
               showBikeMarkings="1" showLinkDecals="1" showLinkRules="1"
               showRails="1" hideConnectors="0" widthExaggeration="1"
               minSize="0" showDirection="1" showSublanes="1"
               spreadSuperposed="0" edgeParam="EDGE_KEY" laneParam="LANE_KEY"
               vehicleParam="PARAM_NUMERICAL" vehicleScaleParam="PARAM_NUMERICAL"
               edgeData="speed">
            <colorScheme name="uniform">
                <entry color="0,0,0" name="road"/>
                <entry color="140,140,140" name="Sidewalk"/>
                <entry color="15,15,150" name="Waterway"/>
                <entry color="120,120,120" name="no passenger"/>
            </colorScheme>
            <scalingScheme name="default"/>
        </edges>

        <!-- THE LOAD-BEARING BLOCK. colorScheme must stay "given vehicle/type/
             route color" for the vType palette above to be what you see. -->
        <vehicles vehicleMode="0"
                  vehicleQuality="2"
                  vehicle_minSize="1.00"
                  vehicle_exaggeration="1.00"
                  showBlinker="0"
                  drawLaneChangePreference="0"
                  drawMinGap="0"
                  showBTRange="0"
                  showRouteIndex="0"
                  scaleLength="1"
                  drawReversed="0"
                  showParkingInfo="0">
            <colorScheme name="given vehicle/type/route color"/>
            <scalingScheme name="uniform"/>
        </vehicles>

        <junctions junctionMode="0" drawShape="1" drawCrossingsAndWalkingareas="1"
                   junction_minSize="1.00" junction_exaggeration="1"
                   showLane2Lane="0" showJunctionIndex="1" showTLSIndex="0"
                   showInternalJunctions="0"/>

        <additionals addMode="0" add_minSize="1.00" add_exaggeration="1"/>
        <pois poiTextColor="0,0,127" poiTextSize="50"/>
        <polys polyTextColor="0,0,127" polyTextSize="50"/>

        <legend showSizeLegend="1" showColorLegend="0" showVehicleColorLegend="1"/>

    </scheme>

</viewsettings>
